    "抽象纹": "abstract pattern",
    "植物纹样": "Plant patterns",
    "莲、牡丹、菊、梅、兰、缠枝藤蔓等花草入纹，六朝起随佛教东传与文人审美兴盛，寄托四季、富贵与清节之意。宝相花是最具代表性的集大成者——它并非自然界实有之花，而是莲花、牡丹、忍冬等花形经艺术综合后的理想化纹样，取\"宝相庄严\"之义，盛唐时最盛。缠枝纹以波状主茎串联花头，寓意生生不息、绵延不断。": "Flowers and plants — lotus, peony, chrysanthemum, plum, orchid and interlocking vines — entered ornament from the Six Dynasties onward, flourishing with the eastward spread of Buddhism and literati taste and carrying meanings of the four seasons, wealth and integrity. The baoxiang flower is the most representative synthesis: not a flower found in nature, but an idealised pattern combining lotus, peony and honeysuckle forms, taking the meaning of “solemn Buddha likeness” and at its height in the High Tang. Interlocking scroll patterns string flower heads along a wavy main stem, symbolising endless vitality and continuity.",
    "依据《中国纹样史》分类体系；宝相花释义参见《中国纹样史》唐代植物纹章节。真迹图版见 Jones 原书青花缠枝、珐琅团花、宝相花诸版。": "Based on the classification system of A History of Chinese Ornament; on the baoxiang flower, see the Tang plant-pattern chapter of the same work. Original plates from Jones — blue-and-white interlocking scroll, enamel roundel and baoxiang flower designs.",
