    "动物纹样": "Animal patterns",
    "鱼、虫、鸟、兽入纹，多取谐音吉语：鱼谐\"余\"（年年有余）、蝠谐\"福\"（福从天降）、鹿谐\"禄\"（加官进禄）、鹤喻长寿、鸳鸯喻和美。动物纹的吉祥化在明清达到高峰，形成\"图必有意、意必吉祥\"的表达习惯。补子纹样还按品级使用：文官用禽、武官用兽，仙鹤为文一品，鹦鹉、鸳鸯等为文七品，四爪为蟒、五爪为龙。": "Fish, insects, birds and beasts enter ornament, often through auspicious homophones: fish (yu) for “surplus” (abundance year after year), bat (fu) for “blessing” (blessings from heaven), deer (lu) for “emolument” (advancement in office), crane for longevity, mandarin ducks for harmony. The auspicious turn of animal patterns peaked in the Ming and Qing, forming the habit of expression that “every picture has a meaning, every meaning is auspicious”. Rank badges also used patterns by grade: civil officials used birds, military officials beasts; the Manchurian crane was first-rank civil, parrots and mandarin ducks seventh-rank civil; four claws were pythons, five claws dragons.",
    "依据《中国纹样史》分类体系与明清补子品级制度；Jones 原书动物题材仅图版 29、38、40、56、64、86 六版，已全部入库归类。": "Based on the classification system of A History of Chinese Ornament and the Ming–Qing rank-badge system; Jones's original has only six animal plates (29, 38, 40, 56, 64, 86), all catalogued and classified.",
    "动物": "animal",
